417 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The applicable TDS for VDAs also contradicts global best practices. Table 2 shows a comparative assessment of the tax structure for VDAs in India and selected other countries. None of the countries, except India, levy TDS on VDA transactions. A TDS of 1 percent \(on transactions above INR 10,000\) is quite high for an industry characterised by high frequency transactions, similar to securities markets. Unlike India, several countries have developed internal mechanisms to track VDA transactions, especially to ensure tax compliance. For example, the Canada Revenue Agency \(CRA\), the US and the UK have collaborated with centralised VDA exchanges to obtain investor information to track VDA investments without jeopardising investor liquidity.)
/E (The applicable TDS for VDAs also contradicts global best practices. Table 2 shows a comparative assessment of the tax structure for VDAs in India and selected other countries. None of the countries, except India, levy TDS on VDA transactions. A TDS of 1 percent \(on transactions above INR 10,000\) is quite high for an industry characterised by high frequency transactions, similar to securities markets. Unlike India, several countries have developed internal mechanisms to track VDA transactions, especially to ensure tax compliance. For example, the Canada Revenue Agency \(CRA\), the US and the UK have collaborated with centralised VDA exchanges to obtain investor information to track VDA investments without jeopardising investor liquidity.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [33]
>>
endobj
418 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (A natural side effect of the TDS is that investors are moving from domestic VDA markets to their offshore counterparts, as we demonstrate in the next section using data on P2P trades. This jeopardises the government's twin goals of bringing transparency to the VDA ecosystem and limiting speculative investment in this space. Conversely, setting the TDS at the same rates as transaction taxes for securities and commodities \(between 0.01 percent and 0.1 percent\) may help maximise reporting and compliance. )
/E (A natural side effect of the TDS is that investors are moving from domestic VDA markets to their offshore counterparts, as we demonstrate in the next section using data on P2P trades. This jeopardises the government's twin goals of bringing transparency to the VDA ecosystem and limiting speculative investment in this space. Conversely, setting the TDS at the same rates as transaction taxes for securities and commodities \(between 0.01 percent and 0.1 percent\) may help maximise reporting and compliance. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
